WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.740
Peace be upon you and welcome to the third video of the second module in our fellowship.

00:05.160 --> 00:10.340
In the previous video, we asked why people get sick, and we learned that the story is not just genes and prescriptions.

00:10.720 --> 00:15.580
Today, we want to dive a step deeper to reveal the hidden forces that determine our health without us realizing it.

00:16.100 --> 00:17.300
Let us ask ourselves an important question:

00:17.800 --> 00:23.240
Do all human beings truly get born with the exact same opportunities to live a healthy and wholesome life?

00:23.240 --> 00:26.480
Suppose with good intentions that we all started from the same starting line,

00:26.480 --> 00:30.420
and that each person has their mind in their head, knows their own path, and has their body in their own hands.

00:30.820 --> 00:33.660
Yet the reality we see on the street tells another story:

00:34.060 --> 00:39.740
The city where you open your eyes, the neighborhood you grow up in, the roof of the house protecting you, and your parents' education level:

00:40.100 --> 00:42.000
all of these are not ordinary marginal details.

00:44.540 --> 00:46.700
These are the true forces that shape your health.

00:47.060 --> 00:51.220
In public health, we call them the social determinants of health.

00:51.220 --> 00:55.000
The World Health Organization summarized these determinants in simple words:

00:55.320 --> 00:59.900
They are the circumstances in which we are born, grow, live, work, and age.

01:00.280 --> 01:03.640
Meaning, health is not fabricated only in doctors' offices and emergency halls.

01:03.960 --> 01:07.700
Real health is cooked in the home kitchen by the quality of food available,

01:08.040 --> 01:12.540
in the school yard by the level of ventilation, in the safety of work environments, and in the cleanliness of the neighborhood.

01:12.960 --> 01:14.620
Let us take education as an example:

01:14.620 --> 01:19.680
Education is not merely a framed certificate hanging on the wall that we photograph next to in order to make our mother happy;

01:20.060 --> 01:21.640
education is a living preventive shield:

01:22.100 --> 01:25.860
It enables you to read food packaging labels and understand what hydrogenated fats mean,

01:26.120 --> 01:30.280
comprehend preventive advice, and opens doors to stable and safe employment.

01:30.500 --> 01:34.020
Investing in education is a direct investment in the arteries of society.

01:34.400 --> 01:37.440
From here, we arrive at income and financial capacity:

01:37.820 --> 01:41.100
Imagine a father who knows well the harms of fried foods and processed oils,

01:41.100 --> 01:44.700
and wishes to bring his children fresh fruits, salmon, and avocados every day.

01:44.940 --> 01:48.820
Yet when he opens his wallet, he discovers that the wallet contains the echo of voice and economic drought.

01:49.180 --> 01:51.380
So he is forced to buy cheap and filling food,

01:51.680 --> 01:53.120
meaning potatoes and bread,

01:53.380 --> 01:55.000
just to silence his children's hunger.

01:55.340 --> 01:58.660
Here the problem is not lack of awareness, nor that the man does not care for his children's health;

01:59.000 --> 02:03.240
the problem is a harsh economic barrier locking the door in the face of any healthy choice.

02:03.240 --> 02:06.500
Excuse me, when a kilogram of apples competes with the price of an oil barrel,

02:06.680 --> 02:09.400
how do you expect to convince people to eat five fruit servings a day?

02:09.400 --> 02:14.240
And when we look a bit beyond money, we find other factors like housing, work environment, and social support.

02:15.080 --> 02:20.040
A damp and overcrowded home with no sunlight entering creates an ideal environment for asthma and chest diseases,

02:20.360 --> 02:23.960
compared to a ventilated house fronted by a green space for children to play.

02:23.960 --> 02:29.060
The work environment: long working hours, harsh treatment, and absence of job security

02:29.060 --> 02:33.200
leave a heavy mark, raising blood pressure and blood sugar. Even social support:

02:33.200 --> 02:38.060
your family and friends who reassure you function like an invisible psychological pharmacy,

02:38.440 --> 02:42.180
giving you immunity and resilience against anxiety, strokes, and life stresses.

02:42.360 --> 02:46.200
To connect this talk with reality, let us hear the story of two people in their forties:

02:46.600 --> 02:50.900
Both completed forty years on the same day, and both started developing hypertension.

02:50.900 --> 02:55.020
The first has higher education, an excellent and stable income,

02:55.020 --> 02:59.520
lives in a quiet neighborhood, has time to walk in the afternoons, has comprehensive health insurance,

02:59.520 --> 03:01.220
and his family supports him and calms his mind.

03:01.520 --> 03:02.800
His condition is completely sound.

03:03.220 --> 03:06.180
As for the second, he works two jobs that grind him down,

03:06.180 --> 03:11.200
returns late at night to a crowded, polluted neighborhood, drowning in debts that prevent him from sleeping,

03:11.480 --> 03:13.180
with no one around him to listen to his worry or support him.

03:13.180 --> 03:17.540
Are you surprised that the illness will progress in the second person in a far worse manner?

03:17.540 --> 03:18.580
They cannot be equal:

03:18.800 --> 03:21.640
The first has social and economic shields protecting him,

03:21.640 --> 03:24.880
while the second stands stripped and exposed before the disease.

03:25.160 --> 03:26.840
And this story takes us to a painful reality:

03:27.220 --> 03:31.540
Climate change is never neutral; climate does not strike everyone with the same justice.

03:31.800 --> 03:35.320
On the contrary, it directs its harshest blows at the most vulnerable groups.

03:35.320 --> 03:42.280
Imagine a suffocating heatwave: The well-off person sits under the air conditioner, a cold glass of water in hand, completely insulated from the heat.

03:42.520 --> 03:48.460
While workers on duty in construction and agriculture stand under a sun burning stone just to secure their daily bread.

03:48.860 --> 03:52.700
And when they return home, they are under a zinc roof without air conditioning or a fan.

03:53.120 --> 03:57.880
When fresh water diminishes, poor neighborhoods are the first to confront thirst and water contamination.

03:58.280 --> 04:02.260
Climate change here is not just heat; it is a multiplier of social injustice.

04:02.260 --> 04:07.640
This brings us to the health educator's true compass: Treat the cause, not just the symptom.

04:07.980 --> 04:11.260
Instead of approaching people with canned advice and entering in a condescending mood:

04:11.940 --> 04:15.720
"Colleagues, you must relax, stay away from stress, and subscribe to a gym,"

04:15.720 --> 04:21.200
the intelligent educator pauses and asks: Are the gym and healthy food accessible to these people in the first place?

04:21.200 --> 04:22.680
What conditions suffocated them?

04:22.680 --> 04:26.260
The goal is to design realistic initiatives filled with compassion and understanding for their circumstances,

04:26.260 --> 04:29.860
not advice floating in the air that increases their sense of helplessness and guilt.

04:29.960 --> 04:32.000
In conclusion, think with us on this question:

04:32.000 --> 04:38.700
What are the social and economic determinants pushing people in your community toward health problems like smoking or diabetes?

04:38.700 --> 04:41.360
Reflecting on this question is the key to real change.

04:41.540 --> 04:45.200
In the fourth video, we will transition together to field assessment tools:

04:45.200 --> 04:50.600
How to monitor and measure these challenges in our neighborhoods accurately to build successful health interventions on the ground.

04:50.600 --> 04:53.100
Thank you, and we will see you in the next video.
